## Step 6: Create a JUnit test

Run:

```
nano src/test/java/com/example/AppTest.java
```

Replace its contents with:

```
package com.example;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class AppTest {

    @Test
    void stringShouldRemainUnchanged() {
        String original = "Hello";
        String modified = original.concat(" World");

        assertEquals("Hello", original);
        assertEquals("Hello World", modified);
    }
}
```

Save and exit.

## Step 7: Create `.gitignore`

Run:

```
cat > .gitignore <<'EOF'
target/
dest.txt
*.log
EOF
```

## Step 8: Build and run the project

Build:

```
mvn clean package
```

Run the application:

```
java -jar target/mavenapp-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar
```

Expected output:

```
=== String Immutability ===
Original: Hello
Modified: Hello World

=== File Copying ===
File copied successfully.
```

Verify the destination file:

```
cat dest.txt
```

Compare the files:

```
diff source.txt dest.txt
```

No output from `diff` means the files match.

## Step 9: Configure Git

Set your Git identity. Replace the example values:

```
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

Initialize Git:

```
cd ~/lab/mavenapp
git init
git branch -M main
```

Add the files:

```
git add .
```

Check the staged files:

```
git status
```

Commit:

```
git commit -m "Create Java Maven string and file copy project"
```

## Step 10: Create a GitHub repository

1. Open https://github.com/new
2. Enter repository name: `mavenapp`
3. Choose Public or Private.
4. Leave **Add a README**, `.gitignore`, and license unselected.
5. Click **Create repository**.

Connect your local project to GitHub. Replace `YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME` with your actual GitHub username:

```
cd ~/lab/mavenapp

git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME/mavenapp.git

git push -u origin main
```

If GitHub requests authentication, use a supported method such as a personal access token or SSH key.

Verify your repository in the browser:

```
https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME/mavenapp
```

## Step 11: Create the Jenkins pipeline file

Run:

```
cd ~/lab/mavenapp
nano Jenkinsfile
```

Paste:

```
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                echo 'Repository checked out by Jenkins.'
            }
        }

        stage('Build and Test') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn -B clean package'
            }
        }

        stage('Run Application') {
            steps {
                sh 'java -jar target/mavenapp-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar'
            }
        }

        stage('Verify File Copy') {
            steps {
                sh 'test -f source.txt'
                sh 'test -f dest.txt'
                sh 'diff -u source.txt dest.txt'
                echo 'Source and destination files match.'
            }
        }
    }

    post {
        success {
            archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar', fingerprint: true
            echo 'Pipeline completed successfully.'
        }

        failure {
            echo 'Pipeline failed. Check Console Output.'
        }
    }
}
```

Save and exit.

Commit and push the pipeline:

```
git add Jenkinsfile
git commit -m "Add Jenkins pipeline"
git push origin main
```

## Step 12: Install Jenkins

Install Java and required tools if you have not already done so:

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install fontconfig openjdk-21-jdk git maven wget -y
```

Add the official Jenkins repository:

```
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings
```

```
sudo wget -O /etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc \
https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2026.key
```

```
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/" \
| sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null
```

Install Jenkins:

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install jenkins -y
```

Start Jenkins:

```
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
sudo systemctl start jenkins
```

Check its status:

```
sudo systemctl status jenkins --no-pager
```

Retrieve the initial administrator password:

```
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
```

Open Jenkins in your Ubuntu browser:

http://localhost:8080

1. Enter the initial administrator password.
2. Click **Install suggested plugins**.
3. Create your administrator account.
4. Complete the setup.

